"White Dwarf Cooling | VirtualDemos",
"White Dwarf Cooling",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (heat capacity and equipartition: \\(\\tfrac{3}{2}kT\\) per particle) and Vol. {#}, §{#} (blackbody radiation and the Stefan–Boltzmann law). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#} and https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Hot young white dwarf; Mestel cooling law; Crystallization begins; Heavier dwarfs crystallize first; Helium vs carbon/oxygen; Cosmic clock.",
"Three panels: the white dwarf’s luminosity (or core temperature) against its cooling age, a slice through the star showing where its ions have frozen, and a schematic HR diagram of the kind Gaia measures. The age line, the dot on the HR diagram and the handle on the star’s surface (its mass) can all be dragged. The cooling age is on a logarithmic scale.",
"The star’s structure is a zero temperature white dwarf held up by degenerate electrons, fixed as it cools. Its core is at one temperature \\(T_c\\). A thin envelope insulates it and sets \\(L \\propto M T_c^{3.5}\\). There is no fusion: the star shines by losing the heat stored in its ions.",
"The ions can be an ideal gas (\\(\\tfrac{3}{2}k\\) per ion) or a Coulomb liquid (more heat per ion). With freezing on, the ions crystallize where the coupling \\(\\Gamma\\) passes {#}, and each ion that freezes can give up a latent heat of \\(0.77\\,kT\\).",
"This is a textbook model. Its envelope is cruder than real ones, so its ages and the luminosity at which freezing starts are only indicative: read its trends, not its exact numbers. Neutrino cooling of very hot white dwarfs is left out.",
"Record reading stores the mass, composition, model, cooling age, luminosity, core and surface temperatures, the solid fraction, and the star’s radius, central density, the temperature at which its center freezes and when and where crystallization begins. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the first data table below, or to the second when the readings are at several masses; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Measure how a white dwarf, a star with no fuel, fades as it loses its stored heat, test Mestel’s cooling law, and find the condition under which its core freezes into a crystal.",
"<strong>Stored heat.</strong> A white dwarf’s core holds \\(N\\) ions at temperature \\(T_c\\). As an ideal gas they store \\(U = \\tfrac{3}{2}NkT_c\\). The electrons hold the star up, but because they are degenerate they store almost no heat.",
"<strong>The envelope.</strong> The heat leaks out through a thin outer layer whose opacity makes",
"for a constant \\(C\\). With \\(dU/dt = -L\\) this gives Mestel’s cooling law: after the star has forgotten its starting temperature,",
"\\[t = \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\frac{U}{L}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad L \\propto t^{-7/5}.\\]",
"<strong>Freezing.</strong> The ions repel each other. Their coupling compares the Coulomb energy of neighbors with their thermal energy:",
"\\[\\Gamma = \\frac{\\langle Z^{5/3}\\rangle e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\,a_e\\,kT},\\qquad a_e = \\left(\\frac{3\\mu_e m_u}{4\\pi\\rho}\\right)^{1/3},\\]",
"where \\(a_e\\) is the radius of the sphere holding one electron, \\(\\mu_e = 2\\) is the mass per electron in atomic mass units, and \\(\\langle Z^{5/3}\\rangle = 25.03\\) for the demo’s carbon/oxygen mix. The ions freeze where \\(\\Gamma\\) passes a critical value \\(\\Gamma_m\\). Since \\(\\Gamma \\propto \\rho^{1/3}/T\\), the center, where the density is highest, freezes first.",
"Constants: \\(k = 1.3806\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K, \\(e^2/4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 = 2.3071\\times10^{-28}\\) J·m, \\(m_u = 1.6605\\times10^{-27}\\) kg, \\(L_\\odot = 3.828\\times10^{26}\\) W, \\(\\sigma = 5.670\\times10^{-8}\\) W/(m²·K⁴), {#} yr \\(= 3.156\\times10^7\\) s.",
"A {#} \\(M_\\odot\\) carbon/oxygen white dwarf holds \\(5.24\\times10^{55}\\) ions. At a core temperature of \\(1.63\\times10^7\\) K, how much heat do they store as an ideal gas? If its luminosity is \\(5.36\\times10^{-3}\\ L_\\odot\\), what age does Mestel’s law give?",
"If a white dwarf takes {#} Gyr to fade to some luminosity, how old is it when it is {#} times fainter?",
"A {#} \\(M_\\odot\\) white dwarf has a radius of {#} km. Find its surface temperature when \\(L = 3.3\\times10^{-5}\\ L_\\odot\\). Would a {#} \\(M_\\odot\\) white dwarf, twice the mass, have a radius larger than {#} km, about the same, or smaller? Note your guess and its reason.",
"<strong>Mestel’s law.</strong> Press the <strong>Mestel cooling law</strong> setup, then <strong>Pause</strong> (it starts playing). Record readings at five cooling ages from {#} Myr to {#} Gyr: {#} Myr, {#} Myr, {#} Gyr, {#} Gyr and {#} Gyr (log₁₀ \\(t\\) = {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}); Analysis {#} uses the {#} Gyr reading. For each: ({#}) set the <strong>Cooling age t (log scale)</strong> slider (drag close, then the arrow keys move log₁₀ \\(t\\) by {#}; the age shows beside the slider); ({#}) press <strong>Record reading</strong>. Then press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> and <strong>Clear</strong>.",
"<strong>Real ions.</strong> Press the <strong>Hot young white dwarf</strong> setup, then <strong>Pause</strong> (it starts playing). Without changing the <strong>Mass M</strong> slider ({#} M☉), record readings at the same five cooling ages, then press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> and <strong>Clear</strong>; they go into a second copy of the first table.",
"<strong>Freezing and mass.</strong> Press the <strong>Heavier dwarfs crystallize first</strong> setup. Set the <strong>Mass M</strong> slider to each mass from {#} to {#} \\(M_\\odot\\) in steps of {#} \\(M_\\odot\\) (the arrow keys move it {#}; the value shows beside it) and press <strong>Record reading</strong> at each; the cooling age does not matter for this part. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: readings at several masses fill the Freezing and mass table.",
